.docname {Build System}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Pin | One exact version written in a file | `python = "3.14.8"` |
    | `PATH` | The list of folders the shell searches for a program | `/usr/bin`, `~/.cargo/bin` |
    | Ambient tool | A tool found on the machine's own `PATH`, installed by someone else | A Node from `nvm` |
    | Activation | A shell hook that puts the pinned tools on `PATH` whenever the shell is inside the repo | `mise activate bash` |
    | `mise exec` | Run one command with the pinned tools first on `PATH` | `mise exec -- cargo test` |
    | Backend | Where `mise` downloads a tool from | `ubi:moonrepo/moon` is a GitHub release |
    | Project | One package registered with `moon` | `telemetry` at `backend/telemetry` |
    | Task | A named job of one project | `build`, `test`, `lint` |
    | Target | Project and task written together | `telemetry:test`; `:test` means every project's `test` |
    | Inputs | The files a task depends on | `src/**/*`, `Cargo.toml` |
    | Hash | A fingerprint of the command, its inputs and its environment | `6bcfbfb3…` |
    | Cache hit | Same hash as a passed run, so the old result is replayed | 8 ms |
    | Affected | A task with at least one changed input file | Editing `src/main.rs` affects `telemetry:test` |
    | Inherited task | A task defined once per language, given to every project of that language | `cargo clippy` for every Rust project |
    | Recipe | A named list of commands in a `just` file | `affected` in `just/test.just` |
    | Module | A file of recipes, reached by its name | `test` is `just/test.just` |
    | Dependency | A recipe that runs before another | `dev` runs `env` first |
    | Parameter | A value passed to a recipe | `service` in `just stack logs telemetry` |
    | `[doc]` | The one-line help text `just --list` shows | "Run only tests for services affected…" |
    | `[private]` | A recipe hidden from the list | `check-version` |

Three tools build Ambitwin. Each has one job:

| Tool | Job | Config |
|---|---|---|
| [mise](#part-1-versions-mise) | Installs every language and CLI at a pinned version | `.mise.toml` |
| [moon](#part-2-tasks-moon) | Runs build, test, lint and deps per project, with cache and affected detection | `.moon/`, each `moon.yml` |
| [just](#part-3-commands-just) | The commands a person types | `Justfile`, `just/*.just` |

`just` is the only door for a person. `just` calls `mise exec`, and `mise exec` runs `moon` or a language tool with the pinned versions.

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        P["person"] --> J["just"]
        J --> M["mise exec"]
        M --> Mo["moon"]
        Mo --> L["cargo, go, npm, uv"]
        J --> D["docker compose"]
        J --> K["kubectl, k3d"]
        J --> Q["quarkdown"]

GitHub Actions calls the language tools directly, without `moon` or `just`, but reads the same pins. See [GitHub Actions CI](ci.qd).

---

## Part 1: Versions (mise)

`mise` installs every language and CLI Ambitwin uses, at the version pinned in `.mise.toml`. It puts exactly those versions on `PATH` for a command. One file answers "which version?" for every machine and for CI.

### The pins

```toml
[tools]
node = "24"
python = "3.14.8"
rust = "1.99.1"
go = "1.27.3"
"ubi:moonrepo/moon" = "2.6.4"
uv = "0.13.5"
istioctl = "1.31.3"

[env]
QUARKDOWN_VERSION = "2.7.0"
```

| Pin | Used for | Also read by |
|---|---|---|
| `node = "24"` | `frontend`, `sensor-simulator`, `eslint-config` | Only the major is pinned: `mise` takes the newest 24, today 24.21.0 |
| `python = "3.14.8"` | `agent`, simulators, `ap-collector`, `scripts/*.py` | CI reads `.python-version` instead |
| `rust = "1.99.1"` | The six Rust services and four schema crates | CI's `setup-rust` action; `cd-registry.yml` builds the Rust base image with it |
| `go = "1.27.3"` | The four Go services and three Go libraries | CI's `setup-go` action; the `golang` tag in the four Go `Dockerfile`s must match by hand |
| `ubi:moonrepo/moon` | `moon` itself, from its GitHub release | |
| `uv` | Python packages and virtual environments | |
| `istioctl` | `just k8s istio-install` | |
| `QUARKDOWN_VERSION` | Not a tool: an environment variable | `just docs build` and the docs workflows compare the installed Quarkdown to it |

Quarkdown is not a `mise` tool. Its release is a 92 MB zip with its own Java runtime, and the `mise` backends keep only the launcher script, which then cannot find its classes. So it is installed separately, and only its version lives here.

### How a command gets its tools

Every `just` recipe that calls a pinned tool starts with `mise exec --`. Here is what that does for `mise exec -- cargo --version`:

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant S as shell
        participant Mi as mise
        participant F as mise.toml
        participant St as mise store
        participant C as cargo
        S->>Mi: mise exec -- cargo --version
        Mi->>F: read the tools section
        F-->>Mi: rust 1.99.1, go 1.27.3, node 24, ...
        Mi->>St: is each version installed?
        alt missing
            Mi->>St: download and install it first
        end
        Mi->>Mi: new PATH with each tool's folder first
        Mi->>Mi: set RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN=1.99.1 and similar
        Mi->>C: start cargo with that environment
        C-->>S: cargo 1.99.1
        Note over C: every child of cargo inherits the same environment

| Step | What happens | Real value on a developer machine |
|---|---|---|
| 2–3 | `mise` finds `.mise.toml` by walking up from the current folder | `/home/…/ambitwin/.mise.toml` |
| 4 | Each tool sits in its own folder in the store | `~/.local/share/mise/installs/rust/1.99.1` |
| 6 | Those folders go first on `PATH`, ahead of anything installed by hand | |
| 7 | Rust is handed to `rustup` through an environment variable | `RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN=1.99.1` |
| 8–9 | The command runs | `cargo 1.99.1` |
| note | Children inherit it: `moon`, then `cargo`, then build scripts | `mise exec -- moon --version` gives `moon 2.6.4` |

**Example.** A laptop has Rust 1.85 from `rustup` as its default.

- `cargo build` in a plain shell uses 1.85. A newer `clippy` lint present in CI does not fire.
- `mise exec -- cargo build` uses 1.99.1, the same as CI.
- `which cargo` can still print `~/.cargo/bin/cargo` under `mise exec`: that is `rustup`'s launcher, and `RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN` picks the version behind it.

### Two ways to get the tools

| Way | When | How |
|---|---|---|
| `mise exec --` | Recipes, scripts, anywhere a shell setup cannot be assumed | Wraps one command |
| Activation | Typing commands by hand | `mise activate bash`, `zsh`, `fish` or `pwsh` in the shell profile |

Activation is optional. A fresh clone with only `just` and `mise` installed runs every recipe, because each one wraps its tool.

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        A["a command needs a tool"] --> B{"run by a just recipe?"}
        B -- yes --> C["recipe already uses mise exec"]
        B -- no --> D{"shell has mise activated?"}
        D -- yes --> E["pinned tool on PATH"]
        D -- no --> F["ambient tool, maybe the wrong version, maybe missing"]
        F --> G["prefix with mise exec --"]

### Who uses mise, and who does not

| Caller | Uses `mise` | How it gets versions |
|---|---|---|
| Every `just` recipe calling `moon`, `uv`, `python3`, `npx`, `istioctl` or a script | Yes | `mise exec --` |
| `moon` and every task it runs | Yes, through its parent | Inherited `PATH` |
| `docker`, `k3d`, `kubectl`, `quarkdown` | No | Installed by hand; not managed |
| CI per-language workflows | No | `setup-rust` and `setup-go` read `.mise.toml` with `grep`; `setup-node` asks for `24.x`; `setup-python` reads `.python-version` |
| `ci-acceptance.yml` | Yes | `jdx/mise-action` |
| Docker images | No | Their own base image tags, kept equal to the pins by hand |

### Changing a version

A version bump is its own commit, because CI reads the same numbers and lint rules can change with them.

1. Edit `.mise.toml`.
2. `mise install`.
3. For Go: change the `golang` tag in the four Go `Dockerfile`s too.
4. For Python: change `.python-version` too.
5. `just lint all` and `just test all`: a new `clippy` or `go vet` can fail code that passed before.

---

## Part 2: Tasks (moon)

`moon` runs the per-project tasks of the whole repository: install, build, test, lint, audit. It knows every project, skips what did not change, and replays results it already has.

Version 2.6.4, pinned in `.mise.toml`, always started through `mise exec`.

### Why moon

| Without it | With it |
|---|---|
| A script loops over 25 folders, one language at a time | `moon run :test` runs every project's `test`, in parallel |
| Every run starts from zero | An unchanged task is replayed in milliseconds |
| "Test what I touched" means remembering which folders | `--affected` asks git |
| Each project repeats its build and test commands | Commands are written once per language and inherited |

### Configuration

| File | Holds |
|---|---|
| `.moon/workspace.yml` | The list of projects, and git as the source of changes, default branch `master` |
| `.moon/toolchain.yml` | Only: Node's package manager is `npm`. No versions: those are `mise`'s |
| `.moon/tasks/rust.yml`, `go.yml`, `typescript.yml`, `python.yml`, `javascript.yml` | The tasks each language gives its projects |
| `<project>/moon.yml` | The project's language, and only what differs from it |
| `.moon/cache/` | Hashes, outputs, the last run report. Not committed |

### The 25 projects

| Language | Projects |
|---|---|
| `rust` | `chat`, `digital-twin`, `notification`, `socket`, `dashboard`, `telemetry`, `telemetry-schema`, `claims-schema`, `twin-schema`, `notification-schema` |
| `go` | `claims-gateway`, `tenancy`, `registry`, `provisioner`, `auth-contracts`, `auth-middleware`, `auth-policy` |
| `typescript` | `frontend`, `sensor-simulator` |
| `python` | `agent`, `ap-simulator`, `aq-simulator`, `ap-collector`, `acceptance` |
| `javascript` | `eslint-config` |

A project missing from `.moon/workspace.yml` does not exist for `moon`: no install, no test, no lint.

### Tasks per language

| Task | `rust` | `go` | `typescript` | `python` | `javascript` |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `build` | `cargo build` | `go build ./...` | `npm run build` | | |
| `test` | `cargo test` | `go test ./...` | `npm test` | `npm test` | |
| `lint` | `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` | `gofmt -l` must be empty, then `go vet ./...` | `npm run lint` | `npm run lint` | |
| `lint-fix` | | | `npm run lint:fix` | `npm run lint:fix` | |
| `deps` | `cargo fetch` | `go mod download` | `npm ci` | `uv sync --locked` | `npm ci` |
| `deps-check` | `cargo check` | | | | |
| `audit` | `cargo audit`, failure allowed | | `npm audit --audit-level=high --omit=dev` | `uv audit` | |
| `relock` | | | delete lockfile, `npm install --package-lock-only --cpu=x64 --os=linux` | `uv lock` | same as typescript |

- `deps`, `relock`, `audit`, `lint-fix` and every `test-integration` have `cache: false`: they change files or depend on the outside world, so a replay would lie.
- Python's `test` and `lint` call `npm`: `backend/agent/package.json` forwards them to `uv run pytest` and `ruff` plus `pyright`. Projects with no `package.json` replace them with `uv` scripts.
- Every `test` lists `/schemas/fixtures/**/*` and `/schemas/json/**/*` as inputs. A leading `/` means "from the repo root". Contract tests read those files, so a fixture edit must re-run them.

### What projects change

| Project | Change | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `chat`, `notification` | `test` = `cargo test --lib --test architecture_fitness` | The other `tests/*.rs` need MongoDB or Redis |
| `socket`, `telemetry` | `test` = `cargo test --lib --test architecture` | Same reason |
| `dashboard`, `digital-twin` | `test` = `cargo test --lib` | Same reason |
| Those six | add `test-integration` = `bash test-integration.sh` | Starts throwaway databases, runs every test, removes them |
| `registry`, `tenancy` | add `test-integration` = `go test -tags=integration -count 1 ./...` | Postgres through testcontainers |
| `frontend` | own `build` and `test` inputs, `test` = `npm run test:unit`, `lint` inputs track `eslint.config.ts` | Vite and Vitest |
| `ap-simulator`, `aq-simulator`, `ap-collector` | `test`, `lint`, `lint-fix` as `uv` scripts | The root `.gitignore` ignores `package.json` |
| `agent` | `test` gets `TESTCONTAINERS_RYUK_DISABLED=true` | Ryuk cannot start under rootless podman |
| `acceptance` | inherited `test`, `lint`, `lint-fix` excluded | Its suite needs a full stack: `just test integration` |

**Merging.** A project's task is merged with the inherited one. `mergeArgs: replace` and `mergeInputs: replace` replace the inherited value instead of adding to it. `frontend` replaces its `test` inputs, so it lists the fixture folders again itself.

### What moon does on `affected`

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant J as just
        participant Mi as mise
        participant Mo as moon
        participant G as git
        participant Ca as moon cache
        participant T as cargo, go, npm, uv
        J->>Mi: mise exec -- moon run :test --affected
        Mi->>Mo: start with pinned tools
        Mo->>Mo: read workspace.yml, each language tasks file, every moon.yml
        Mo->>Mo: build the graph: 25 projects, their tasks
        Mo->>G: which files changed?
        G-->>Mo: local changes in the working tree
        Mo->>Mo: keep each test whose inputs match a changed file
        loop each kept task, in parallel
            Mo->>Mo: hash command, args, env, input file contents
            Mo->>Ca: seen this hash with a pass?
            alt hit
                Ca-->>Mo: replay stored output
            else miss
                Mo->>T: run the command in the project folder
                T-->>Mo: exit code and output
                Mo->>Ca: store the hash and output, if it passed
            end
        end
        Mo-->>J: summary, non-zero exit if any task failed

| Step | What happens | Example |
|---|---|---|
| 3–4 | Reads the config into one graph | 25 projects |
| 5–6 | Asks git for changed files. By default these are the local, uncommitted changes | `backend/agent/evals/dataset.json` |
| 7 | Keeps only tasks whose `inputs` match | `agent:test` watches `app/**/*`, `tests/**/*`, `pyproject.toml`: the dataset is none of them, so it is skipped |
| 9 | Hashes everything the result depends on | `6bcfbfb3…` |
| 10–11 | Hit: prints `(cached, 6bcfbfb3)` | 8 ms |
| 12–14 | Miss: runs the tool, saves the result | 5.9 s |
| 16 | Any failure fails the whole run | |

**Real numbers.** `moon run auth-contracts:lint`, the same files both times:

| Run | Output | Time |
|---|---|---|
| First | `auth-contracts:lint (5s 902ms, 6bcfbfb3)` | 5.9 s |
| Second | `auth-contracts:lint (cached, 6bcfbfb3)` | 8 ms |

**Real affected example.** With uncommitted edits to `backend/notification/CLAUDE.md`, `backend/chat/openapi.yaml` and `backend/agent/evals/dataset.json`, `moon query affected` lists projects `notification`, `chat` and `agent` as touched. No `test` or `lint` is affected: none of those files is a test or lint input. Only `agent:lint-fix` and `agent:relock` are, because they declare no inputs and so match any file of their project.

### Will a task run?

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        S["target, like :test"] --> A{"--affected given?"}
        A -- no --> H
        A -- yes --> B{"a changed file matches its inputs?"}
        B -- no --> Skip["skipped"]
        B -- yes --> H{"cache: false?"}
        H -- yes --> Run["run"]
        H -- no --> C{"same hash passed before?"}
        C -- yes --> Rep["replay, a few ms"]
        C -- no --> Run
        Run --> P{"exit code 0?"}
        P -- yes --> Save["save to the cache"]
        P -- no --> Fail["run fails"]

`--force` skips the cache and the affected check.

### Inspecting without running

```bash
mise exec -- moon query projects            # every project, its language
mise exec -- moon query tasks               # every task of every project
mise exec -- moon task telemetry:test       # one task: command, inputs, where it came from
mise exec -- moon query affected            # what the current changes touch
mise exec -- moon query changed-files       # the files moon sees as changed
```

### Adding a project

1. Register it in `.moon/workspace.yml`.
2. Add `moon.yml` with its `language`, and only the changes it really needs.
3. Register it in `.github/services.json` too, for CI.
4. Check: `mise exec -- moon query projects`.

.box {Pick the language on purpose} type:{warning}
    The language decides the inherited tasks. `javascript` gives only `deps` and `relock`, for support packages. A Python project with no `package.json` must replace `test` and `lint`, or they fail calling `npm`.

---

## Part 3: Commands (just)

`just` is the only command line a person needs. Every workflow is `just <module> <recipe>`. A recipe is a short, named wrapper around the real command, so nobody has to remember the long one.

### Why just

| Without it | With it |
|---|---|
| `mise exec -- moon run :test --affected` typed by hand | `just test affected` |
| Commands kept in a README that drifts | The recipe is the command; `just --list` is always current |
| A `Makefile` | No file-timestamp rules to fight, readable errors, arguments and modules built in |
| One way per language | One door for Rust, Go, TypeScript, Python, Docker, Kubernetes and docs |

### Layout

The root `Justfile` only declares modules and the shell:

```just
set shell := ["bash", "-c"]
set windows-shell := ["powershell.exe", "-NoLogo", "-Command"]

mod setup 'just/setup.just'
mod stack 'just/stack.just'
mod test 'just/test.just'
mod db 'just/db.just'
mod agent 'just/agent.just'
mod docs 'just/docs.just'
mod lint 'just/lint.just'
mod k8s 'just/k8s.just'
```

Each module file starts with `set working-directory := '..'`, so every recipe runs from the repo root, whatever folder `just` was typed in. Bare `just` runs `just --list`.

| Module | Recipes | What they drive |
|---|---|---|
| `setup` | `install`, `clean-install`, `audit`, `deps-check` | `mise install`, `moon` |
| `stack` | `env`, `dev`, `start`, `down`, `logs` | `scripts/env/*.sh`, `scripts/compose/compose-run.py`, `docker compose` |
| `test` | `all`, `affected`, one per project, `<svc>-integration`, `frontend-e2e`, `agent-integration`, `eval`, `integration` | `moon`, `uv`, Playwright, `backend/acceptance/run-integration-tests.sh` |
| `lint` | `all`, `affected`, `fix`, `workflows` | `moon`, `scripts/check-workflow-registry.py` |
| `db` | `clear`, `clear-telemetry` | `mongosh` and `psql` inside the running containers |
| `agent` | `ingest` | `scripts/ops/ingest-docs.py` |
| `docs` | `build`, `preview`, `serve` | Quarkdown, `scripts/check-docs-links.sh`, `scripts/docs-meta.py` |
| `k8s` | `create`, `setup`, `apply`, `validate`, `secrets`, `build`, `push`, `load`, `restart`, `logs`, … | `k3d`, `kubectl`, `istioctl`, `docker` |

`just --list <module>` shows one module, like `just --list k8s`.

### Recipes that run others first

A recipe can name others to run first.

| Recipe | Runs first | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `just lint all`, `just lint affected` | `workflows` | A workflow with a broken `needs:` fails on GitHub with no logs at all; this catches it locally |
| `just stack dev`, `just stack start` | `env` | The stack needs `.env` |
| `just db clear`, `clear-telemetry` | `runtime` | Needs `compose.runtime.yml`, written by the last `just stack` run |
| `just docs build` | `check-version`, `check-layers` | Quarkdown must match `QUARKDOWN_VERSION`; Quarkdown's DOM names may appear only in two files |
| `just docs serve` | `build` | Serves what was just built |
| `just k8s setup` | `create`, `gateway-api`, `istio-install` | A cluster first, then its parts |
| `just k8s build-push` | `build`, `push` | |

### Example: `just stack dev --no-agent`

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant J as just
        participant E as env recipe
        participant Sc as scripts/env
        participant Py as compose-run.py
        participant D as docker compose
        J->>E: dependency first
        E->>Sc: create.sh, vapid.sh, token.sh, config.sh, ...
        Sc-->>E: env file filled, existing keys kept
        J->>Py: mise exec -- python3 scripts/compose/compose-run.py dev --no-agent
        Py->>Py: collect docker-compose.yml and docker-compose.dev.yml of each folder
        Py->>Py: drop folders matching agent
        Py->>Py: write compose.runtime.yml with an include list
        Py->>D: remove stopped leftovers of the same containers
        Py->>D: docker compose -f compose.runtime.yml up --watch --remove-orphans, minus agent
        D-->>J: services running, files watched

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| 2 | Eleven scripts, each adds only the keys `.env` lacks: VAPID keys, eval token secret, Keycloak, Langfuse, control plane, gateway signing key, ingest key, telemetry database, Rust settings |
| 5 | Root `docker-compose.yml`, then each folder in `backend/`, `simulators/`, `devices/`, then `frontend/`. In `dev` mode each folder's `docker-compose.dev.yml` is added |
| 6 | `--no-agent` is a group: it drops every name containing `agent`. Bare words work too: `just stack dev socket` drops `socket` |
| 7 | `compose.runtime.yml` is generated and ignored by git. `just db` and `just stack logs` read it |
| 9 | `dev` adds `--watch`, so file changes reach the containers. `start` uses `up --build -d` instead |

Groups: `--no-agent`, `--no-metrics` (Prometheus, Grafana), `--no-simulators`, `--no-devices` (`ap-collector`). `--build` rebuilds images, `--dry-run` prints the command and stops.

### Rules for recipes

- **A pinned tool always goes through `mise exec --`.** `docker`, `k3d`, `kubectl` and `quarkdown` are not pinned and are called directly.
- **A recipe that repeats per project belongs in `moon`**, not here. `just` only names it.
- **Scripts are POSIX `sh` or standard-library Python.** No Node in `scripts/`.
- **Every public recipe has a `[doc]`.** That line is the only help `just --list` shows.

---

## One command, end to end: `just test affected`

The three tools in order. The person types one line; the rest runs underneath.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant P as person
        participant J as just
        participant Mi as mise
        participant Mo as moon
        participant T as cargo, go, npm, uv
        P->>J: just test affected
        J->>J: find module test, load just/test.just
        J->>J: working directory = repo root
        J->>Mi: mise exec -- moon run :test --affected
        Mi->>Mo: moon with pinned tools on PATH
        Mo->>Mo: keep tests whose inputs match a changed file
        Mo->>T: run each kept task, or replay it from cache
        T-->>Mo: exit code
        Mo-->>Mi: exit code
        Mi-->>J: same exit code
        J-->>P: stop at the first failing line

| Step | Tool | What happens |
|---|---|---|
| 2–4 | `just` | Finds the module, moves to the repo root (the folder above `just/`) |
| 5 | `just` | The recipe body is one line, run by `bash -c` |
| 6 | `mise` | Puts the pinned tool versions first on `PATH` |
| 7–9 | `moon` | Picks the affected tests, hashes them, runs or replays them |
| 10 | `just` | A line that exits non-zero stops the recipe. Later lines do not run |

---

## Traps

### mise

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `uv: command not found` | A command ran outside `mise` | `mise exec -- uv …`, or activate `mise` |
| Local lint passes, CI fails | A different Rust or Go ran locally | Run through `just` or `mise exec` |
| `mise WARN mise version … available` | `mise` itself is older than the latest | Harmless; `mise self-update` |
| `link.exe not found` (Windows) | Rust needs the MSVC linker | Install Visual Studio Build Tools, "Desktop development with C++" |
| Linker errors on Linux or macOS | No C compiler | `build-essential`, or `xcode-select --install` |
| `quarkdown … installed, but .mise.toml pins …` | Quarkdown version differs from `QUARKDOWN_VERSION` | Install the pinned build |

### moon

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| A fixture changed but tests still "pass" from cache | A project replaced its `test` inputs and dropped `/schemas/fixtures/**/*` | List the fixture folders in the replaced inputs, like `frontend` does |
| A Go integration run says `[no test files]` and passes | A packed `go test -tags=integration …` string loses the tag | Write `command: go` and the flags as `args`, as `registry` and `tenancy` do |
| An integration suite passes without touching Docker | Go served a cached pass | `-count 1` in the args |
| `just test all` fails with no database running | A project's `test` runs `tests/*.rs` that need infra | Narrow its `test`, move the rest to `test-integration` |
| A new service is never tested | Missing from `.moon/workspace.yml`, or wrong `language` | `moon query projects` |
| `just setup install` reinstalls everything | Expected: `deps` has `cache: false` and runs without `--affected` | |

CI does not run `moon`. Each workflow calls the language tools directly, with the same commands: [CI](ci.qd).

### just

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| A recipe fails on Windows | `windows-shell` is set, but many recipes use `bash` syntax: `test -f`, `sed`, `#!/usr/bin/env bash` bodies | Run from WSL or Git Bash |
| A recipe works locally but CI says the `Justfile` does not parse | `just` is not pinned: CI installs 1.42.4, a laptop may have a newer one with newer syntax | Keep to features 1.42.4 knows; CI runs `just --summary` to catch it |
| `compose.runtime.yml not found` | `just db …` before any `just stack` run | `just stack dev` first |
| Recipe runs in the wrong folder | A module file without `set working-directory := '..'` | Add it |
| `command not found` inside a recipe | The tool is pinned but the line lacks `mise exec --` | Add the prefix |

Each module's recipes in use: [Run the Application](../setup/running.qd).
